  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 331 AM CST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nEarly this morning, water vapor satellite and upper air analysis \nshow a rather amplified pattern across the CONUS with potent deep \nlayer troughing across the Midwest and Appalachian Mountains \nand a mid/upper ridge over the Great Basin and Desert Southwest.\nAt the surface, a ridge is sinking southward into the central \nplains as another push of cooler air approaches the forecast \narea. With slightly drier air and a persistent northerly drift\nearly this morning, the probability of patchy fog is a bit \nlower than previously expected. Still, sparse coverage of fog, \nmainly near creeks and rivers, will be possible through 15Z this\nmorning. With a back door cold front side swiping the forecast \narea today, a modest increase in northerly winds is forecast \nthis afternoon with sustained winds around 10 to 15 mph, and \ngusts up to 25 mph at times. As the surface ridge sinks further \nsouth, winds will decrease again tonight. This along with clear\nskies will allow optimal radiational cooling, and temperatures \nwill dip to or below freezing by early Friday morning. With a \npost frontal airmass gripping the region on Friday, afternoon \nhighs will only rise into the 50s area-wide. Light winds and \nclear skies will again support temperatures around or below \nfreezing Saturday morning.\n\nMid range global guidance continues to show a negatively tilted \ntrough digging over the central and southern plains this weekend, \nand this feature will likely be the catalyst for rain chances late \nSaturday night through Sunday night. One caveat to potential rain \nchances on Sunday will be the track of this system. Guidance has \ntrended just a tick further south over the past 24 hours, and it's \nentirely possible only southern and southeast Kansas see rainfall as \nthis system passes through the region. Regardless, any rain \nthat falls will be light, and limited instability should prevent\nthe potential for thunder. Cooler temperatures will persist \ninto the weekend with highs in the 40s, and lows in the 30s \nacross the area. Should precipitation continue into Sunday \nnight, temperatures should remain well above freezing keeping \nthe precipitation type limited to rain. Heading into next week, \nthe return of southerly low-level flow will promote a gradual \nwarming trend with afternoon highs returning to the 60s by mid \nweek. Lastly, long range deterministic and ensemble guidance \ncontinues to show the potential for a series of systems passing \nthrough the area mid to late next week. This would generally \nincrease the potential for precipitation across the region in \nthis time frame, and details will come into better focus over \nthe next few days.",
